**Read:** [Advanced fluid dynamics — Mei notes and MIT modules](../sources/Advanced-Fluid-Dynamics-Mei-MIT.lingua#r_21NXVH78F0NYDPDVTG66PG261P), chapter 2 sphere flow and Oseen correction; [Fluid Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_3SP698XHQFSZZ6N9Q22NESHMEA), chapter 3 Stokes flow and swimming; [Fluid mechanics films and illustrated notes — NCFMF](../sources/NCFMF-Fluid-Mechanics-Films.lingua#r_0RT6J26HJJ83MX74030JPEHQVP), low-Reynolds-number flow and accompanying notes.

When inertia is negligible on the relevant length and time scales, the incompressible equations reduce to −∇p+μ∇²u=0 and ∇·u=0. Their linearity permits superposition for fixed geometry and linear boundary conditions. Reversing the imposed motion reverses velocity and hydrodynamic force in this ideal limit; diffusion, boundaries that change their configuration and non-Newtonian memory can change the wider problem.

For an isolated rigid sphere of radius a translating slowly through an unbounded Newtonian fluid, the drag magnitude is 6πμaU. Balance it against buoyant weight to obtain the small-particle settling estimate U=2(ρ_p−ρ)ga²/(9μ), then recompute the Reynolds number to check the assumption. Walls, other particles, fluid inertia, nonsphericity and surface mobility require further treatment.

Follow the complete sphere solution and reciprocal-theorem argument in the readings. Compare force, velocity and dissipation formulations. Explain why the analogous unbounded two-dimensional cylinder problem encounters the Stokes paradox and why an apparently small inertial correction can matter far from the body. Use that failure to test the spatial uniformity of an approximation.

